item.description:
Take the next step in your fundraising career
Lauderdale House is looking for an experienced fundraiser who is ready for a role with more responsibility and the opportunity to help shape how fundraising develops across the organisation.
This is our first dedicated fundraising role, but you won't be starting from scratch. We have successful previous trust fundraising, established education and community programmes, previous individual donors and a large community of people with a long-standing connection to Lauderdale House.
Working closely with the Director, you'll help develop fundraising into a consistent and sustainable income stream, with a particular focus on Trusts & Foundations andIndividual & Legacy Giving.
You'll develop and manage a pipeline of trust and foundation applications, write compelling applications and reports, build relationships with funders and develop approaches to encourage longer-term support.
You'll also help establish our individual and legacy giving programme, researching and cultivating prospective donors, making fundraising approaches, developing donor stewardship and putting appropriate systems in place for donor data and Gift Aid.
Alongside this, you'll contribute to fundraising strategy, impact and evaluation, cases for support, fundraising communications, reporting and compliance.
About you
We're looking for someone with solid practical fundraising experience and a track record of securing income who is ready to take the next step in their career and possesses all or most of the following:
a successful track record in fundraising relevant to this role, preferably in arts, heritage and/or community organisations;
experience managing your own fundraising workload or portfolio with a good degree of independence;
the confidence to use your judgement, make recommendations and move work forward without extensive day-to-day supervision;
experience taking responsibility for fundraising targets or income goals;
a strong understanding of fundraising trends, especially within the arts, heritage and community sectors;
an understanding of the role of fundraising in organisational growth;
knowledge of fundraising regulations, data protection, ethical standards and best practice;
experience using CRM systems, databases or other tools for data mining and supporting prospecting;
strong interpersonal and relationship-building skills and the confidence to develop relationships with funders, donors and prospective supporters;
the ability to work collaboratively with colleagues who aren't fundraising specialists;
excellent verbal and written communication skills;
the ability to influence people and inspire trust;
strong organisational skills and the ability to manage multiple deadlines and priorities;
creative and lateral problem-solving skills and the ability to deal with the unexpected;
a positive attitude and willingness to help where needed;
confident use of Microsoft Office and the ability to learn other relevant software;
excellent and accurate attention to detail;
an interest in arts, heritage, education and/or community activity and the impact organisations such as Lauderdale House can have; and
a commitment to equality and diversity.
You don't need to have previously been a Head of Fundraising or run an entire fundraising function. You might currently be a Fundraising Officer, Senior Fundraising Officer, Development Officer or similar and be ready for a role with more responsibility and scope to use your own judgement.
Above all, we're looking for someone who combines fundraising knowledge and real experience with initiative. You won't be expected to arrive knowing everything about Lauderdale House, but you should have enough fundraising experience to recognise opportunities, recommend a course of action and confidently take responsibility for delivering it.
About Lauderdale House
Lauderdale House is a Grade II* historic house in Waterlow Park, Highgate, brought to life as a busy arts, heritage, education and community centre. Around 80,000 people visit us each year, and in 2028 we will celebrate 50 years as an arts and education centre.
Salary: £37,818
Hours: Full-time, 35 hours per week
Contract: Permanent
Holiday: 25 working days plus public holidays
Location: Lauderdale House, Waterlow Park, Highgate N6 – on-site, not suitable for remote or hybrid working
How to apply
Please send your CV and a covering letter to Director Katherine Ives, with the subject ‘Application for Fundraising Manager’.
Your covering letter should explain why the role interests you and how your experience meets the essential requirements. We'd particularly like to hear about fundraising work you have personally been responsible for and examples of income you have successfully helped to secure.
Please also include contact details for two referees and let us know at what point they may be contacted. We will not contact referees without your permission.
For an informal conversation about the role, please contact Director Katherine Ives.
We welcome applications from people from all backgrounds and are happy to discuss reasonable adjustments during the recruitment process.
Application deadline:
9am on 5 October
First interviews:
w/c 12 October
Second interviews:
w/c 19 October

Role Purpose:
As a Senior Debt Adviser, you will work with, support, assist and represent vulnerable people of all ages and abilities to improve their financial situation and wellbeing. You will assess people’s circumstances and advise them accordingly. This will involve offering support and advice in income maximisation, financial capability, energy comparison, welfare benefit and specialist debt advice.
You will work with, support, and represent people of all ages and abilities, many of whom will be experiencing circumstances that make them vulnerable.
The role will involve delivering support and casework with a multi-channel approach. Some outreach visits may also be required.
Key Responsibilities:
· Working with and supporting vulnerable people of all ages and abilities to try to maximise their income and improve their financial circumstances and wellbeing. You will establish a relationship based on trust to ensure you provide the necessary support and assistance;
· Providing technical supervision to support the Debt Advice team in their roles to ensure the service is accurate, effective and tailored to the individuals’ circumstances.
· Actively seek ways of assisting the Management Team in the management of the Debt Advice service. This includes contributing to thinking about development of systems, services and staff.
· To conduct monthly case reviews of the Auriga Assist Debt Team in line with established quality standards and provide feedback with team members.
· To be the first point of contact for the Auriga Assist Debt Team on complex issues.
· Develop and manage interviews with clients using sensitive listening and questioning skills to allow clients to explain their problem(s);
· Complete an initial assessment of a client’s needs to identify areas where support is required and plan next steps/ provide support throughout the process;
· Helping people to maximise their income, provide guidance on budgeting, provide unbiased and holistic advice and guidance on how to deal with problem debt, liaising and negotiating with third parties, including statutory and non-statutory organisations as required;
· Acting on behalf of the client wherever necessary to facilitate debt relief options to include form filling, drafting, or writing letters and telephoning, supporting clients to plan, implement, and review action and provide continuing support to clients where necessary, including assistance with disputes and complaints;
· Enabling clients to act on their own behalf and empower them to set their own priorities
· Actively encourage engagement from clients referred through to the service from both internal and external sources;
· To carry a caseload of enquiries requiring detailed ongoing casework. To maintain accurate and appropriate records relating to each case, recording all activity, interventions and outcomes within the case record whilst ensuring that correct procedures are followed, to ensure quality standards are met;
· Manage your own workload, administration, and diary commitments;
· To ensure a high-quality service to customers including, but not limited to, face to face, telephone, written and emails;
· Collaborate with colleagues, stakeholders and health and social care professionals to evaluate caseload needs and the progress that has been made;
· To keep up to date and share knowledge with the team on legislation, case law, policies and procedures and undertake appropriate training as and when required;
· Able to proactively identify own training and development needs and those of others;
· Ability to work within and adhere to organisational policies and procedures and ensure work meets internal and external SLAs;
· Perform other duties commensurate with the post as required.
Requirements:
· Educated to GCSE level (or equivalent by experience);
· At least 2 years’ experience in the advice sector;
· A good working knowledge of welfare benefits and charitable trust funds; and basic knowledge of services provided by the NHS and social care services

Reporting to:
Principal
Line managing:
Site Manager, Professional Services team (Student Recruitment and Communications Manager and Office staff), IT Manager
Key Relationships:
Senior Management Team, Head of Access to Learning, the Chair and Trustees of the City & Guilds of London Art School, Chair and Trustees of the City and Guilds Art School Property Trust
Main purpose of role:
The Director of Operations is an important, senior role at the Art School, responsible for supporting the Principal in the delivery of the Strategic Vision and to monitor the progress of its objectives. They are responsible for the efficient and effective management of internal resources and stakeholders, and will act as the deputy to the Principal in all matters excluding academic. They will also contribute to the oversight of recruitment of students and staff.
The Director of Operations provides vision and professional leadership of registry, professional services, IT, admissions, as well as the management of compliance, HR, and the Art School’s technical tutors. They have some oversight of marketing inasfar as it relates to recruitment. The Director of Operations is the key liaison with the Property Trust, which is the official landlord for the Art School and the governing body that oversees the main buildings at our site in Kennington. They are the lead person, responsible for the OfS return and so it is anticipated that the successful candidate would have a level of quality assurance experience and familiarity with policy oversight.
The Art School is an independent educational charity with approximately 300 students and 100 members of staff. The Art School is entering a new strategic planning cycle that will see us growing our profile and portfolio; expanding our international links and external partnerships; and, deepening our civic impact on our neighborhood in Kennington and further afield.
MAIN DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES
The main duties and responsibilities of the post are:
Leadership and Strategy
Supporting the Principal in the delivery of the Strategic Vision for the Art School
Working closely with the Head of Finance to allocate resources in line with the Art School improvement plan
Oversee the management of and communication with, internal stakeholders of the School
Compliance and Governance
Ensure that the systems and processes employed by the Art School comply with legal, audit and regulatory requirements,
Oversee Risk Management, including Health & Safety compliance;
Champion and oversee EDI across the Art School;
Monitor the Art School’s performance against strategic objectives;
Attend meetings of the Board of Trustees and relevant Committees, providing effective and timely reporting, proactive advice and support;
Have overall responsibility for operational strategy including and policy management;
To be the Art School ‘point of contact’ to the Property Trust;
Manage legal and related matters as and when they arise.
Registry
Oversee the team that handles student registry, academic administration and affairs, compliance and all non-academic matters of student experience;
Ensure the accurate and timely submission of statutory and regulatory returns (e.g. to the Office for Students, Higher Education Statistics Agency, Office of the Independent Adjudicator for HE, Student Loans Company, UKVI etc);
Act as the primary liaison for the Art School’s validating partners (Arts University Bournemouth, University of the Arts London Awarding Body);
Act as Secretary to the Academic Board and oversee the arrangements for Art School Examination Boards;
Operations
Implementation of HR processes and systems, seeking external advice as appropriate;
Recruitment and line management of operational staff;
Lead the process for the Art School’s annual staff appraisal scheme (Individual Performance Review) recommending associated staff development and training activities as agreed with the Principal;
Day-to-day oversight of the professional teams managing the Estate, Technical Resources and IT infrastructure, and lead strategic planning relevant to these areas;
Chair the Art School’s Site and Environment Committee;
Manage the relationship with the Property Trust on a day-to-day basis.
External Profile and Recruitment
Supporting the Principal to ensure successful student recruitment, working collaboratively with other academics;
Development of the Art School’s activities (including commercial activities), short courses and profile;
Oversee the Health & Safety and other logistical aspects of public events such as Degree Show
Additional Duties and Responsibilities
Deputise where appropriate for the Principal;
Lead and undertake Health and Safety duties and responsibilities appropriate to the role;
Undertake other duties of a reasonable nature, as may be determined by the post holder’s line manager from time to time, in consultation with the post holder.
It is the duty of the post holder not to act in a prejudicial or discriminatory manner towards members of staff, students, visitors or members of the public. The post holder should also counteract such practice or behaviour in others by challenging and reporting it.
This is a description of the job as it is presently constituted. It is the Art School’s practice to periodically review job descriptions to ensure that they accurately reflect the job that is required to be performed, or to incorporate proposed reasonable changes following negotiation with the postholder.

About Degrees
The Degrees Initiative is a UK-based NGO that builds the capacity of the Global South to evaluate solar radiation modification (SRM) geoengineering, a controversial proposal for reducing some impacts of climate change by reflecting sunlight away from the Earth. Degrees is neutral on whether SRM should ever be used, but we believe that the Global South should be empowered to conduct their own research and to play a central role in SRM discussions. The initiative has been working in different forms for over a decade, and our work receives worldwide coverage and widespread acclaim.
Role
The Events Officer will coordinate and support the planning and delivery of Degrees’ international events, including workshops bringing together Degrees-funded researchers, policymakers and other stakeholders, and the biennial Degrees Global Forum. Working closely with colleagues across the organisation, the Events Officer will help ensure high-quality events that strengthen collaboration, showcase research outcomes, facilitate engagement with policymakers and advance Degrees’ mission to build capacity in the Global South to engage equitably in SRM deliberations.
The Events Officer will also provide general administrative support to the Policy Engagement team.
This role is ideal for a highly organised individual who enjoys coordinating complex logistics, managing multiple priorities, improving processes and ensuring that events run smoothly.
Key responsibilities
The Events Officer works under the supervision of the Events & Projects Manager, while supporting the Policy Engagement team in cooperation with the Policy Engagement Director. Responsibilities will include:
Events
Arrange travel, lodging and other travel logistics for, funded scientists, policymakers, and other external participants;
Maintain event budgets and expenditure records, working with the Events & Projects Manager and Operations and Finance team to monitor expenditure against budget;
Arrange meeting facilities;
Facilitate requests for visas and other travel authorisations;
Serve as a point of contact for external event participants’ queries about travel and events;
Assist Degrees staff with travel questions;
Ensure proper distribution of participant documents in a timely manner;
Maintain accurate event plans, timelines, trackers, action logs and participant records, and proactively follow up outstanding actions;
Coordinate communications and follow-up actions between the Events & Projects Manager and other Degrees teams;
Support supplier management and contracting processes as directed by the Events & Projects Manager;
Support the development and maintenance of appropriate event risk assessments;
Support post-event close-out, including participant feedback, reconciliation of records and expenditure, documentation and lessons learned.
Degrees Global Forum
Support the Events & Projects Manager and the Conference Manager in the planning and delivery of the Degrees Global Forum;
Coordinate participant registration processes and attendee records;
Support abstract, poster and session submission processes;
Support coordination with the venue, destination management company and other event suppliers, including maintaining schedules, requirements and action logs;
Coordinate travel and accommodation arrangements for funded participants.
Support of policy engagement team
Maintain the list of external policy engagement contacts.
Draft general and formal correspondence; reviewing and formatting documents and slides according to style;
Organise and maintain the policy engagement team’s shared mailbox and filing system;
Schedule staff and other meetings;
Support the administration and maintenance of Policy Engagement team processes, including agreed monitoring, evaluation and learning systems;
Key relationships
Events & Projects Manager (line manager)
Policy Engagement team, especially its Director
Conference Manager
Programmes team
Fundraising Manager
Operations and Finance team
external venues, travel providers, and other suppliers
Qualifications
Essential
At least two years’ relevant experience in administration, project coordination, events, or a similar role;
Experience supporting the delivery of events, workshops, conferences, or similar activities, including coordinating travel and other logistics;
Bachelor’s degree in a relevant field, or an additional three years of relevant professional experience;
Strong organisational and project management skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities and deadlines and work effectively under pressure;
Strong attention to detail and a professional, responsible approach to work;
Ability to work independently with minimal supervision in a dynamic, remote environment;
Strong written and verbal communication skills in English;
Strong interpersonal skills and an ability to work effectively with colleagues and external stakeholders from a wide range of backgrounds;
Excellent Microsoft 365 skills, particularly Excel, SharePoint, Word, PowerPoint, Teams and Outlook, and confidence learning new digital tools;
Experience maintaining financial records, monitoring or reconciling budgets, and working with spreadsheets;
Experience using CRM systems, project management tools or similar databases;
Ability to travel internationally when required, including to attend the biennial Degrees Global Forum; and
Legal right to work in the United Kingdom.
Desirable
Experience supporting international conferences, academic events, or other complex multi-day events;
Experience with event registration or conference management systems;
Experience using travel management platforms and supporting visa or travel-authorisation processes;
Experience developing or maintaining standard operating processes;
Knowledge of project management methodologies;
Advanced digital skills, such as specialised spreadsheet functions, database administration or online form and workflow tools;
Experience working in the charity, international development, research, non-governmental or intergovernmental sectors;
One or more additional languages; and
An interest in climate change, international development or international research collaboration.
Benefits of working at Degrees
You will join a growing and dynamic charity operating at the intersection of climate change and global development.
You will have the opportunity to contribute to a potentially high-impact issue by helping shape global conversations on climate science, empowering researchers in the Global South, and collaborating with some of the world’s leading SRM experts.
The role is fully remote, allowing you to live anywhere within the United Kingdom. The charity has no central office and offers each employee up to £250 per month (including VAT) to cover co-working space costs. The team meets face-to-face at quarterly ‘awaydays’ in the UK, which are organised and paid for by the charity.
Degrees provides an 8% employer pension contribution with no required employee contribution, which is generous by the standards of the UK third sector.
The role includes opportunities for international travel.
You will receive 25 days of annual leave per year, in addition to UK public holidays. If the charity closes between Christmas and New Year, you may also receive up to three extra days of leave.
Personal development budget of £1,000 per year.
